INT. SUNSET BARN - WEDDING AFTERPARTY - NIGHT

String lights sway over a dance floor littered with confetti and shoes. A half-eaten cake leans like a tower in Pisa. The DJ booth glows, empty, still thumping a bass line nobody is dancing to.

On the cake table, a plastic bride-and-groom topper wobbles. Then, impossibly, climbs down.

GUS, a wiry corgi in a tiny bow tie, trots in, dragging a garter in his teeth.

BEA, 20s, the bride's sibling, in a rumpled suit, tie loose, hair wild, vaults a chair and skids to a stop in front of him. Bea is out of breath, and they are grinning like a person who has just won something.

BEA
Gus! Drop the garter, buddy. That belongs to the bouquet relay.

Gus drops it. Then snatches it back. BOING, his tail whips a candle holder, and a tower of cups topples with a CRASH.

GUS
Sorry, Captain Bea! The relay's the only game I'm any good at!

BEA
Nobody's saying you're not good. They're saying you cheat.

GUS
I do not cheat. I take shortcuts.

Bea laughs and crouches. They point to the finish line, a ribbon strung between two keg stands at the far end of the barn.

BEA
Okay, Gus-Gus, ground rules. Garter in mouth, no teeth on the ribbon, and nobody bites the best man.

GUS
That was ONE time!

A starter pistol, actually a champagne cork, POPS from the balcony. The crowd, offscreen, ROARS.

Bea sprints. Gus scrambles after them, paws skittering on the polished boards. Over a fallen chair. Under a table. WHOOSH past a sleeping uncle. The garter flutters like a flag.

BEA
Left, Gus, left!

GUS
I know where left is! I'm a professional!

They hit the ribbon together. SNAP. Streamers rain down. Gus tumbles into Bea's arms, and they both go down laughing on a pile of discarded party hats.

BEA
Photo finish. Tie game.

GUS
(panting)
Rematch at the honeymoon?

BEA
Don't push your luck, champ.

Gus puts the garter on Bea's head like a crown. Bea lets him.
